Add a button size option to the window decoration

The decoration took the button side from the title bar height, so a larger
button needed a larger caption font or a larger TitleBarPad value. Breeze
offers a button size option, and this commit adds the same option.

The ButtonSize key in the General group of kwinqtcurverc takes the five names
of kcm_breezedecoration, and an absent or unknown value gives ButtonDefault.
Each size takes the Breeze multiple of the KDecoration3 grid unit: 1, 1.5, 2,
2.5 or 3.5 units. The decoration rounds the side to an odd number of pixels,
so a glyph keeps a centre pixel. It holds every side at 11 pixels or more,
where the glyph strokes stay 2 pixels wide. The title bar grows when a button
needs more room than the caption, and TitleBarPad grows the title bar alone.
At DejaVu Sans 10 pt with no pad the Normal button goes from 15 to 21 pixels.

The glyph side rounds up to the next odd number, so Tiny and Small draw
distinct line art wherever their button sides differ. The context help mark
takes a round join at the four smallest sides, where its corner spike reached
the frame ring.

The settings module gains a "Button size:" box on the General tab, and a save
reloads KWin at once. The help text of "Titlebar text padding:" now states
that the pad changes the title bar alone. Out of scope: a per button size, the
window icon size and the title alignment option of Breeze.

Colour the decoration buttons from the settings

The Plasma 6 decoration reads three flags of the titlebarButtons key
nowhere. The icon colour flag, the standard colour flag and the hover
colour flag therefore change no pixel of a window decoration. The 18 icon
colour pickers also reach no window decoration.

Read the three flags where the decoration picks its colours. Under the
icon colour flag the line art takes a colour of the titlebarButtonColors
key. It reads the second block of the key for an active window and the
third block for an inactive window. Under the hover colour flag the line
art of a button under the pointer takes the hover colour of the colour
scheme. Under the standard colour flag the frame takes the button colour
of the palette.

drawMdiControl() in qt6/style/qtcurve.cpp and QtCurveButton::drawButton()
in qt6/kwin/qtcurvebutton.cpp read the same flags in the same order. A
window decoration therefore matches an MDI window of the style again.

Draw the arrow glyphs and the sunken button ground

The Plasma 6 decoration reads two flags of the titlebarButtons key
nowhere. The arrow flag therefore leaves the minimize glyph and the
maximize glyph unchanged. The sunken background flag only moves the
button frame one pixel inward. It draws no ground, so the promise of a
drawn background goes unmet.

Read the arrow flag in the glyph painter. The minimize button then takes
an arrow that points down. The maximize button takes an arrow that points
up. The restore button takes two frames. drawIcon() in
qt6/style/qtcurve.cpp draws the same three shapes.

Draw the ground of the sunken background flag under each button group,
between the title bar and the buttons. drawSunkenBevel() in
qt6/kwin/qtcurveclient.cpp drew the same gradient over the same area. A
group of one button keeps no ground, as it did there.

Blend the title bar into the window background

The Plasma 6 decoration reads the blend flag of the windowBorder key
nowhere. The option that blends the title bar colour into the background
colour therefore changes no pixel of a window decoration.

Give the last stop of the title bar gradient the window background at no
alpha under the blend flag. The bottom of the title bar then fades into
the fill under it. drawBevelGradientReal() in qt6/style/qtcurve.cpp
clears the last stop of an MDI window title bar the same way.

The reader keeps the blend flag with the colour title bar only flag
alone. That flag paints the window background under the title bar. The
fade therefore always reaches the background colour of the option.

Take the menubar colour for the title bar

The Plasma 6 decoration reads the menubar colour flag of the windowBorder
key nowhere. The option that gives the title bar the menubar colour
therefore changes no pixel of a window decoration.

Build the menubar colour from the shadeMenubars key and return it from
the title bar colour under the flag. setMenuColors() in
qt6/style/qtcurve.cpp builds the same colour for the menubar of an
application, so the title bar and the menubar under it hold one colour.
An inactive window keeps the title bar colour while the shade menubar
only when active key is on, as paintEvent() in
qt6/kwin/qtcurveclient.cpp did.

The window border value of the shadeMenubars key needs no arm, because
the reader drops the flag for that value.

The change sits on the branch work/kdecoration3 of the local tree, as the third
commit and the four commits after it. The fork wyn/qtcurve holds that branch.
Merge request !9 on system/qtcurve holds that branch.

Drop this patch when upstream merges the change.

